# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FS Suite — Canonical production .env
#
# This is the source-of-truth template for the .env you keep in your
# password manager (Bitwarden) and feed into the provisioning scripts.
# Every other surface (EC2 .env, GCP Secret Manager, GitHub Secrets) is
# a derived copy, refreshed from this file by:
#
#   infra/ec2/setup.sh           → propagates everything into EC2
#   infra/cloudrun/setup.sh      → mirrors into GCP Secret Manager
#   infra/bootstrap-github-secrets.sh → syncs cat B+D to GH Secrets
#
# Categories (see infra/README.md → Secrets):
#   A  API runtime         — consumed by the NestJS process
#   B  Frontend build      — injected into Expo web bundle
#   D  Workflow data       — read directly by GitHub Actions workflows
#
# Category C (CI/CD pipeline auth: EC2_HOST/SSH_KEY, GCP_WIF_*,
# CLOUDFLARE_*, TURBO_*) is NOT in this file — it lives only in GitHub
# Secrets because it authenticates the pipeline itself.
#
# All values shown are placeholders. Replace with your real values.
# Never commit a populated copy of this file.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ═══ A · API runtime ════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Database (Supabase) ──
# Supavisor session-mode pooler URL. The pooler is required because
# Supabase's direct endpoint is IPv6-only and our runtimes are IPv4.
# Get from: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Database → Connection string → Session pooler
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project>:<password>@aws-1-eu-central-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?pgbouncer=true

# ── Redis (Upstash) ──
# Use the `rediss://` (TLS) URL. Get from: Upstash console → your database → Details → REST/Redis connect
REDIS_URL=rediss://default:<password>@<host>.upstash.io:6379

# ── Google OAuth ──
# Create at: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# Authorized redirect URI: https://api.fs-suite.com/v1/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<id>.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-<secret>

# ── JWT keypair (RS256) ──
# Used by both EC2 and Cloud Run runtimes — they must share the same
# pair so a token issued by one is accepted by the other.
#
# Operator workflow:
#   - First-time provisioning: leave both blank. `infra/ec2/setup.sh`
#     generates a fresh pair on the EC2 host. After it runs, capture
#     the values back into this file (Bitwarden) and re-run
#     `infra/cloudrun/setup.sh` so GCP Secret Manager picks them up.
#       ssh fs-suite "sudo grep -E '^JWT_(PRIVATE|PUBLIC)_KEY=' /opt/fs-suite/.env"
#   - Subsequent reprovisioning: leave the populated values here.
#     EC2 setup will preserve them (sessions don't get invalidated)
#     and Cloud Run picks up the same pair.
#
# Values are PEM with literal \n escapes between header/body/footer
# (escaped single-line format). Example:
#   JWT_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIE...\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Encryption ──
# AES-256-GCM key (32 bytes hex). Persistent — regenerating breaks all
# encrypted OAuth tokens and BYOK API keys. Generate once with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
ENCRYPTION_KEY=<64-char-hex>

# ── Sentry ──
# Shared by backend (this var) and frontend (injected as
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN at Expo build time). Errors are separable in
# Sentry by SDK tag (`node` vs `javascript`).
# Get from: Sentry → your project → Settings → Client Keys (DSN)
SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.de.sentry.io/<project>

# ── AI providers (free tier) ──
# Gemini: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Groq: https://console.groq.com/keys
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ── Cloudflare R2 (chart cache) ──
# Create the bucket `fs-suite-charts` in Cloudflare R2, then create an
# API token scoped to that bucket.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# ── Weather providers (optional) ──
# OpenWeatherMap: https://openweathermap.org/api  (precipitation tiles)
# AVWX: https://avwx.rest  (enriched METAR decoding)
# Leave empty to disable the corresponding features.
OWM_API_KEY=
AVWX_TOKEN=

# ── Admin metrics endpoint ──
# Header-token auth for GET /v1/admin/metrics. Same value must reach
# EC2 .env, GCP Secret Manager, and the GitHub Secret with the same
# name (the bootstrap script handles GH). Generate once:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
ADMIN_METRICS_TOKEN=<64-char-hex>

# ═══ B · Frontend build ═════════════════════════════════════════════
# These are injected into the Expo web bundle by deploy-app.yml at
# build time. The bootstrap script pushes EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY to
# the GitHub Secret POSTHOG_KEY automatically.

# ── PostHog ──
# Project API key (`phc_` prefix). Designed to be embedded in client
# bundles — public by design, can only ingest events. Get from:
# PostHog → Project Settings → Project API Key
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_<key>

# ═══ D · Workflow data ══════════════════════════════════════════════
# Consumed by GitHub Actions workflows directly (not via the API).
# bootstrap-github-secrets.sh syncs all of these to GH Secrets.

# ── Supabase service role ──
# Service-role JWT (`eyJ...`). Used by db-backup.yml to upload dumps
# to Supabase Storage. NEVER use this in the API runtime — it bypasses
# Row Level Security. Get from: Supabase → Settings → API → service_role secret
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ<...>
